==English==
{{wikipedia}}
[[File:Edmund_blair_leighton_accolade.jpg|thumb|upright|Painting of an '''accolade''' ({{senseno|en|salutation}}).]]
[[File:Image from page 061 of The Century dictionary and cyclopedia (1897) (14598482188).jpg|thumb|upright|'''Accolade''' ({{senseno|en|architecture}}), early 16th century (France).]]

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kʷel-}}
First attested in the 1620s, borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|accolade}}, from {{der|en|oc|acolada||an embrace}}, from {{m|oc|acolar||to embrace}}, from {{der|en|it|accollato}}, from {{der|en|VL.|*accollō||to hug around the neck}}, from {{der|en|la|ad-}} + {{m|la|collum||neck}} (English {{m|en|collar}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæk.ə.ˌleɪd/|/ˌæk.ə.ˈlɑd/|a=RP,US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-accolade.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# An expression of [[approval]]; [[praise]]. {{defdate|from 1852}}
# A special [[acknowledgment]]; an [[award]].
#: {{ux|en|This film is likely to pick up major '''accolades'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=22 May 2016|author=Phil McNulty| title=Crystal Palace 1-2 Manchester United| work=BBC| url=http://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36292265| passage=Rooney led Manchester United up the Wembley steps to collect the FA Cup and add a missing medal to his collection - a richly deserved '''accolade'''.}}
# An [[embrace]] of [[greeting]] or [[salutation]].
#* {{RQ:Scott Waverley|volume=I|page=131|passage={{...}} for the tears stood in the old gentleman's eyes, when, having first shaken Edward heartily by the hand in the English fashion, he embraced him ''a-la-mode Françoise'', and kissed him on both sides of the face; while the hardness of his gripe, and the quantity of Scotch snuff which his '''''accolade''''' communicated, called corresponding drops of moisture to the eyes of his guest.}}
# {{senseid|en|salutation}} {{lb|en|historical}} A [[salutation]] marking the [[conferring]] of [[knighthood]], consisting of an [[embrace]] or a [[kiss]], and a slight blow on the shoulders with the flat of a sword.
#* {{RQ:Twain Prince and the Pauper|passage=“Rise, Sir Miles Hendon, Knight,” said the King, gravely—giving the '''accolade''' with Hendon’s sword—“rise, and seat thyself. Thy petition is granted.  Whilst England remains, and the crown continues, the privilege shall not lapse.”}}
#* {{RQ:Cervantes Ormsby Don Quixote|volume=I|chapter=III|page=130|passage=As he had already told him, he said, there was no chapel in the castle, nor was it needed for what remained to be done, for, as he understood the ceremonial of the order, the whole point of being dubbed a knight lay in the '''accolade''' and in the slap on the shoulder, and that could be administered in the middle of a field;{{...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Jan Morris|title=Spain|publisher=Faber and Faber|isbn=978-0-571-24787-5|year=1964|year_published=2008|chapter=Four Cities|passage=It was in the queer little Church of Vera Cruz, beneath the castle, that the Knights Templar performed their secret rites of chivalry, standing vigil over their arms all night, in all the mysterious splendour of seneschal, gonfalon, and '''accolade'''.}}
# {{lb|en|music}} A [[brace]] used to join two or more [[staves]].
# {{lb|en|US}} Written [[presidential|Presidential]] [[certificate]] recognizing service by military personnel or civilians serving the US armed forces who died or were wounded [[in action]] between 1917 and 1918, or who died in service between 1941 and 1947, or died of wounds received in [[Korea]] between June 27, 1950 and July 27, 1954.
# {{senseid|en|architecture}} {{lb|en|architecture}} An ornament composed of two [[ogee]] curves meeting in the middle, each concave toward its outer extremity and convex toward the point at which it meets the other.
# {{lb|en|typography}} {{synonym of|en|curly bracket}}

